<?php

namespace Illuminate\Support;

/**
 * @template TMethod of string
 * @template-covariant TValue
 * @template-covariant TCollection of \Illuminate\Support\Enumerable
 *
 * The template list is Laravel 13.31+'s `<TMethod, TValue, TCollection>`, declared for every supported
 * Laravel version: the runtime proxy is identical, 12.20-13.30 only lack the annotation. The collection
 * stubs' `@property-read` blocks spell the receiver class out (`Collection<TKey, TValue>`) because a
 * `static` there stays an unparameterized `Collection&static`. TCollection is bounded by bare `Enumerable`:
 * the `Enumerable<array-key, TValue>` bound Laravel declares rejects `Collection<int, X>` (TKey is
 * invariant), and TCollection is covariant so `Enumerable<int, X>` accepts it too (see decisions.md).
 *
 * The @mixin TValue annotation from the Laravel source causes Psalm to resolve method
 * calls on the proxy through the item type, returning the item method's return type
 * (e.g. int). Psalm's stub system merges @mixin annotations rather than replacing them,
 * so this stub cannot remove @mixin TValue from Psalm's internal class storage.
 *
 * The actual false-positive fix (sortByDesc->method()->values()) is implemented in
 * HigherOrderCollectionProxyHandler::afterMethodCallAnalysis(), which intercepts the
 * mixin-resolved call and overrides the return type with the precise collection type
 * (e.g. Collection<TKey, TValue>, bool, float|int|null, etc. depending on the proxy).
 *
 * This stub's purpose is narrower: declaring __call prevents Psalm from reporting
 * UndefinedMagicMethod errors when sealAllMethods is enabled.
 */
class HigherOrderCollectionProxy
{
    /**
     * Proxy a method call onto the collection items.
     *
     * @param  string  $method
     * @param  array  $parameters
     * @return mixed
     */
    public function __call($method, $parameters) {}
}
